schoenberg.tex:
Classical (Torgerson--Gower) MDS can be expressed via spectral decomposition~\cite{borg2005modern}. Let the squared dissimilarity matrix be
\begin{equation}
 \mathbf{G} = -\frac{1}{2} H \mathcal{D}^{(2)} H \,,
\end{equation}
where $H = I - \frac{1}{n}\mathbf{1}\mathbf{1}^{\mathsf T}$ is the centering matrix. Performing the eigendecomposition $\mathbf{G} = U \Lambda U^{\mathsf T}$ and retaining the top $p$ components yields
\begin{equation}
 X = U_p \Lambda_p^{1/2}.
 \label{eq:top_p_eigenvalue}
\end{equation}
This formulation clarifies that MDS coordinates are not arbitrary visualization outputs but approximate Euclidean realizations of topology-derived dissimilarities. In this sense, the embedding is directly tied to the network’s relational structure, which makes repeated realizations of the same optimization problem physically interpretable.

