shape.tex:
Throughout, terminal configurations are compared modulo the Euclidean group
\begin{equation}
\mathrm E(p)=\mathbb R^p\rtimes O(p),
\label{eq:euclidean_group}
\end{equation}
because the stress objective depends on a configuration only through its pairwise distances. Two terminal configurations related by a translation, rotation, or reflection are the same geometry for our purposes, so every quantity we report is built from pairwise distances and no alignment step enters the analysis.

Because the pairwise-distance matrix $D^{(m)}$ of a run is invariant under
$\mathrm{E}(p)$, terminal configurations can be compared without alignment. We
use the scale-free distance
%
\begin{equation}
  \Delta_{mn}
  =
  \left[
  \frac{\sum_{i<j}\left(d_{ij}^{(m)}-d_{ij}^{(n)}\right)^2}
       {\sum_{i<j}\delta_{ij}^{2}}
  \right]^{1/2}.
  \label{eq:run_distance}
\end{equation}
%
which is defined without reference to the curvature of
Sec.~\ref{sec:curvature}. Keeping the two independent matters: a merge threshold derived from $\lambda_{\min}$ would make the grouping a function of the very quantity it is meant to be compared against, and the stopping tolerance of the
optimizer does not supply such a threshold either, being a tolerance on the relative improvement of the stress rather than a bound on the distance to a minimizer. The denominator is fixed by the target graph metric and is shared by all run comparisons for that graph. The grouping distance is therefore independent of the output norms and of the curvature diagnostic.

terminal.tex:
For a fixed graph $G$ with $N\ge2$ labeled nodes, let $\Pi$ denote the embedding protocol, including the initialization law, target dimension $p$, optimization settings, and a prespecified
numerical acceptance event $A_{\rm num}$.
We distinguish the number of independent optimization attempts, $M_{\rm attempt}$, from the number of accepted outcomes, $M_{\rm adm}$.

Let $D$ denote the vector of terminal pairwise Euclidean distances. The acceptance probability and the accepted distance law are
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\alpha_{G,\Pi}
&=\Pr(A_{\rm num}\mid G,\Pi),\\
\mu^{\rm adm}_{G,\Pi}
&=\mathcal L(D\mid A_{\rm num},G,\Pi).
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:accepted_distance_law}
\end{equation}
The conditional law is defined for $\alpha_{G,\Pi}>0$.
We estimate the acceptance probability by
\begin{equation}
\widehat{\alpha}_{G,\Pi}
=\frac{M_{\rm adm}}{M_{\rm attempt}}.
\label{eq:acceptance_rate}
\end{equation}
Rejected attempts contribute to the acceptance-rate calculation but are not assigned zero inconsistency.

hessian.tex:
At a terminal configuration $X^{\star}$ the second variation of the stress
[Eq.~\eqref{eq:stress_function}] is block structured. Writing
$u_{ij}=(x_i-x_j)/d_{ij}$ for the unit vector along a pair and $\mathbb{1}_p$
for the $p\times p$ identity,
%
\begin{equation}
  B_{ij} \;=\; 2\,w_{ij}\Big[\,
     u_{ij}u_{ij}^{\mathsf{T}}
     \;+\;\Big(1-\frac{\delta_{ij}}{d_{ij}}\Big)
        \big(\mathbb{1}_p-u_{ij}u_{ij}^{\mathsf{T}}\big)\Big],
  \label{eq:hess_block}
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
  H_{ii}=\sum_{j\neq i}B_{ij},
  \qquad
  H_{ij}=-B_{ij}\quad (i\neq j),
  \label{eq:hessian}
\end{equation}
%
so $H$ is a graph Laplacian whose edge weights are the $p\times p$ blocks
$B_{ij}$.

We write $\lambda_{\min}$ for the smallest eigenvalue of the projected Hessian and, because $\lambda_{\min}$ carries the scale of $H$ and hence depends on $N$, we report the dimensionless ratio
%
\begin{equation}
  \widetilde{\lambda}_{\min}
  \;=\;\frac{\lambda_{\min}}{\operatorname{tr}(H_{+})/(Np-q)},
  \qquad q=\tfrac{1}{2}p(p+1),
  \label{eq:lam_rel}
\end{equation}
%
where $H_{+}$ denotes the positive part of the projected spectrum.

