We compare the same full set of labeled node pairs across all accepted runs. This set is fixed before observing the outcomes:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal P
=\{(i,j):1\le i<j\le N\},
\qquad
Q=|\mathcal P|=\frac{N(N-1)}{2}.
\label{eq:admissible_pairs}
\end{equation}
Because pairwise distances are invariant under translations, rotations, and reflections, no alignment of the configurations is required.

For the sample statistics below, set $M=M_{\rm adm}$ and reindex the accepted runs by $m=1,\dots,M$.
Each accepted run provides coordinates
$\{\mathbf r_i^{(m)}\}_{i=1}^{N}$ and pairwise distances
\begin{equation}
d_{ij}^{(m)}
=
\left\|
\mathbf r_i^{(m)}-\mathbf r_j^{(m)}
\right\|_2,
\qquad (i,j)\in\mathcal P.
\end{equation}
For $M\ge2$, define the sample mean and divisor-$M$ variance by
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\bar d_{ij}
&=\frac{1}{M}\sum_{m=1}^{M}d_{ij}^{(m)},\\
\sigma_{ij}^{2}
&=\frac{1}{M}\sum_{m=1}^{M}
\left(d_{ij}^{(m)}-\bar d_{ij}\right)^{2}.
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:pairwise_statistics}
\end{equation}

Keff.tex:
Nonzero pairwise variance can arise from within-class spread, separated class means, or both. Under the additional idealization that the accepted law is supported on exactly two pair-distance vectors with probabilities $P$ and $1-P$, let $\Delta_{ij}$ denote their pairwise distance difference. The population variance then reduces to
\begin{equation}
 s_{ij}^2 = P(1-P)\,\Delta_{ij}^2.
 \label{eq:two_basin}
\end{equation}
the product of a Bernoulli occupancy variance $P(1-P)$ and the squared geometric separation. The occupancy factor is maximized at $P=\tfrac12$ but is not the Shannon entropy $-\!\sum_\gamma P_\gamma\log P_\gamma$: for more than two basins the variance depends on the pairwise geometric separations $d_{ij}(X_\gamma^\star)$, so two landscapes with identical basin entropy but different minimizer geometries yield different NEI. Under the idealization that each terminal class corresponds to one exactly converged minimizer, the variance combines class occupancy with geometric separation. Occupancy entropy alone does not determine this variance. For finitely terminated computations, NEI is interpreted as a terminal spread without assuming a one-to-one correspondence between terminal classes and minima.

split.tex:
Because the pairwise-distance matrix $D^{(m)}$ of a run is invariant under
$\mathrm{E}(p)$, terminal configurations can be compared without alignment. We
use the scale-free distance
%
\begin{equation}
  \Delta_{mn}
  =
  \left[
  \frac{\sum_{i<j}\left(d_{ij}^{(m)}-d_{ij}^{(n)}\right)^2}
       {\sum_{i<j}\delta_{ij}^{2}}
  \right]^{1/2}.
  \label{eq:run_distance}
\end{equation}
%
which is defined without reference to the curvature of
Sec.~\ref{sec:curvature}. Keeping the two independent matters: a merge threshold derived from $\lambda_{\min}$ would make the grouping a function of the very quantity it is meant to be compared against, and the stopping tolerance of the
optimizer does not supply such a threshold either, being a tolerance on the relative improvement of the stress rather than a bound on the distance to a minimizer. The denominator is fixed by the target graph metric and is shared by all run comparisons for that graph. The grouping distance is therefore independent of the output norms and of the curvature diagnostic.

Given a grouping $\{\gamma\}$ with occupancies $P_{\gamma}$ and group means
$\mu^{\gamma}_{ij}$, the ensemble variance of a pair separates as
%
\begin{equation}
  \sigma^{2}_{ij}
  \;=\;
  \underbrace{\sum_{\gamma}P_{\gamma}\big(\mu^{\gamma}_{ij}-\bar d_{ij}\big)^{2}}
    _{\text{between}}
  \;+\;
  \underbrace{\sum_{\gamma}P_{\gamma}\,
     \mathrm{Var}_{\gamma}\!\big[d_{ij}\big]}_{\text{within}} ,
  \label{eq:var_decomp}
\end{equation}
%
The term between being the empirical counterpart of the two-configuration
expression Eq.~\eqref{eq:two_basin} without the restriction to two groups. To quantify the between-group fraction of NEI, we apply the same pairwise normalization to both terms:
\[
\beta=
\frac{
\displaystyle\sum_{(i,j)\in\mathcal P}
\frac{\sum_\gamma P_\gamma
(\mu^\gamma_{ij}-\bar d_{ij})^2}{\bar d_{ij}^{\,2}}
}{
\displaystyle\sum_{(i,j)\in\mathcal P}
\frac{\sigma_{ij}^{2}}{\bar d_{ij}^{\,2}}
},
\qquad \mathcal I>0.
\]
For the empirical decomposition, $P_\gamma$ is the accepted-run fraction in group $\gamma$, and each within-group variance uses the group size as its divisor. The fraction is undefined when $\mathcal I=0$.

